Array [
  Object {
    "description": "Create new fiori elements page like ListReport, ObjectPage, CustomPage",
    "functionalityId": "add-page",
  },
  Object {
    "description": "Remove existing fiori elements page from the application",
    "functionalityId": "delete-page",
  },
  Object {
    "description": "Add new controller extension by creating javascript or typescript file and updates manifest.json with entry. Controller extensions allow users to extensiate default behaviour with custom controllers code.",
    "functionalityId": "create-controller-extension",
  },
  Object {
    "description": "Inserts a SAP Fiori elements building block (FilterBar, Chart, Field, Form, Page, Table, RichTextEditor, or an aggregation child: CustomColumn, Action, CustomFilterField, CustomFormField, RichTextEditorButtonGroups) into an existing view or fragment XML file. For FilterBar, Table, Form, Chart: the required annotation shell (com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.SelectionFields, com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.LineItem, com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.FieldGroup, com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.Chart) is written automatically to the app annotation file — you do NOT need to check or read the annotation file first. If the annotation already exists it will not be duplicated. Call execute_functionality exactly once per building block — do NOT retry or call it again after a successful result. If the user request is ambiguous (e.g. multiple elements of the same type exist at the same level — such as two macros:Table or two macros:FilterBar in the same parent — and the target is unclear), ask the user to clarify by id (e.g. \\"which table — Table or Table1?\\") before calling execute_functionality. Do NOT read any files to resolve ambiguity. Use search_docs with a building block type name (e.g. \\"FilterBar building block\\", \\"Table building block\\") to retrieve full property documentation from bb_macro_api.md before populating buildingBlockData. Types requiring UI5 >= 1.117.0: RichTextEditor, RichTextEditorButtonGroups. Types requiring UI5 >= 1.136.0: Page (basic). Types requiring UI5 >= 1.145.0: Page (full layout). All building blocks require an OData V4 service. Other building block types (FilterBar, Chart, CustomFilterField, CustomFormField, Field, Form, Table, CustomColumn, Action) have no specific minimum UI5 version beyond the application baseline. Chart: on UI5 < 1.97 the chart is always generated with contextPath applied. RichTextEditor and RichTextEditorButtonGroups must be placed in a fragment file (custom section), NOT in a view file. If the app has no fragment files, tell the user: \\"RichTextEditor can only be added to a custom section fragment. Please create a custom section fragment first, then ask again.\\" When adding RichTextEditorButtonGroups to a RichTextEditor that already has button groups, always call get_functionality_details again first to get the current aggregationPath — do not reuse a path from a previous call. Page building block must be placed in a custom page view file (.view.xml), NOT in a fragment file. If the app has no custom page view files, tell the user: \\"Page building block can only be added to a custom page view. Please create a custom page view first, then ask again.\\" Only one macros:Page element is allowed per view. If a macros:Page already exists in the view, do NOT add another — instead add building blocks (e.g. Table, FilterBar) inside it using the XPath that points to the macros:Page items aggregation. When adding a Page building block, always use the XPath that is a direct child of /mvc:View as aggregationPath (e.g. /mvc:View/Page) — never use a deeper path like /mvc:View/Page/content. That direct-child-of-/mvc:View rule is ONLY for the Page building block itself. Any OTHER building block (Table, FilterBar, Chart, Form, Field) added to a custom page view that already contains a Page must be inserted INTO the Page's content aggregation — pick the deepest offered XPath ending in the Page content (e.g. /mvc:View/*[local-name()='Page']/*[local-name()='content']), never the bare /mvc:View. The Page layout is chosen with the \\"templateType\\" property (allowed values: \\"full\\" = generate all aggregations, \\"basic\\" = minimal self-closing macros:Page) — the field is \\"templateType\\", not \\"template\\". The Form building block uses the \\"title\\" property for its visible title (not \\"header\\"). Aggregation child types (CustomColumn, Action, CustomFilterField, CustomFormField, RichTextEditorButtonGroups) insert into a parent macro aggregation — their aggregationPath must point to the parent macro element (CustomColumn → Table, Action → Table, CustomFilterField → FilterBar, CustomFormField → Form, RichTextEditorButtonGroups → RichTextEditorWithMetadata). For aggregation children: if the user specifies a position (before/after a specific existing column/field), pass anchor and placement as TOP-LEVEL properties of buildingBlockData — anchor: \\"<existingElementKey>\\", placement: \\"Before\\"|\\"After\\" (do NOT nest them inside a position object) — the anchor value is the key attribute of the existing element in the XML. Keys are always kebab-case derived from the existing element title text (e.g. \\"Test Header\\" → key=\\"test-header\\", \\"Product Name\\" → key=\\"product-name\\"), so you can derive the anchor without reading the file. Otherwise omit anchor/placement and the column/field is appended at the end. The visible title of a CustomColumn goes in the \\"header\\" property; CustomFilterField and CustomFormField use \\"label\\"; an Action uses \\"key\\" (a kebab-case identifier) for its id and \\"text\\" for its button label — do NOT put the identifier in the generic \\"id\\" field. Fragment and event handler options for aggregation children (CustomColumn, Action, CustomFilterField, CustomFormField, RichTextEditorButtonGroups): (1) New fragment, no handler — omit embededFragment entirely; a new fragment file is created automatically. (2) New fragment, with handler — set embededFragment.eventHandler to true (auto-generates a standalone .js handler named after the fragment with a default onPress function) or { fileName: \\"MyHandler\\", fnName: \\"myFn\\" } (auto-generates a standalone .js handler with a specific name and function). Do NOT create handler files manually. (3) Existing fragment, no handler — set embededFragment.name to the existing fragment file name; no handler is generated. (4) Existing fragment, with handler — set embededFragment.name to the existing fragment file name and embededFragment.eventHandler to { fileName: \\"ExistingHandler\\", fnName: \\"myFn\\" }. For NEW fragments (cases 1 and 2), if the user gives the fragment a name, set embededFragment.name to that name so the generated fragment file is created with it — do NOT put the fragment name in the generic \\"id\\" field; if embededFragment.name is omitted the file name is derived automatically. Ask the user which case applies before calling execute_functionality if it is unclear. Action handler options: (1) New handler file, new function — set embeddedAction.eventHandler to { fileName: \\"MyHandler\\", fnName: \\"myFn\\" }. The tool creates the file automatically. (2) Existing handler file, new function — set embeddedAction.name to the existing file and embeddedAction.eventHandler to { fileName: \\"ExistingFile\\", fnName: \\"newFn\\" }. The tool wires the XML but does NOT append the function to the existing file. After execute_functionality, you MUST manually append the new function to the handler .js file — this is the only case where manual handler editing is required; the \\"Do NOT create handler files manually\\" rule in the fragment guide does not apply to Action case (2). (3) Existing handler file, existing function — set embeddedAction.name to the existing file and embeddedAction.eventHandler to { fileName: \\"ExistingFile\\", fnName: \\"existingFn\\" }. The function already exists — no file editing needed. RichTextEditorButtonGroups (buildingBlockType \\"rich-text-editor-button-groups\\"): buildingBlockData must contain a \\"buttonGroups\\" ARRAY — one element per toolbar group: buttonGroups: [{ name: \\"<groupName>\\", buttons: \\"<comma-separated button ids>\\" }, ...]. Each element has \\"name\\" (the group name) and \\"buttons\\" (a single comma-separated string, e.g. \\"bold,italic,underline\\"). Do NOT pass a flat top-level { name, buttons } object or a top-level \\"buttons\\" field — always wrap every group inside the buttonGroups array, even when adding a single group. Adding button groups REWRITES the entire set from the array you pass — it does NOT append to existing groups. So when the target RichTextEditor ALREADY has button groups and you are adding more, first read the current fragment file to list the existing groups (their name + buttons), then pass ALL groups together — existing plus new — in the buttonGroups array, using the macros:RichTextEditorWithMetadata element as the aggregationPath. This is the one case where reading the fragment first is expected. FilterBar wiring: add the FilterBar first (note its id), then when adding the Table pass buildingBlockData.filterBar set to that id. If the Table already exists and needs linking, edit the view XML directly to add filterBar=\\"<filterBarId>\\" to the macros:Table element. metaPath format for metaPath building blocks (Table, FilterBar, Chart, Form) — never set contextPath, always leave it unset: (1) Relative binding (navigation property as value source, e.g. \\"to_Booking\\"): metaPath=\\"<navProp>/@<annotation>#<qualifier>\\" — no leading slash. Example: metaPath=\\"to_Booking/@com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.LineItem#tableMacro\\". (2) Absolute binding (entity set as value source, e.g. \\"Booking\\"): metaPath=\\"/<entitySet>/@<annotation>#<qualifier>\\" — leading slash. Example: metaPath=\\"/Booking/@com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.SelectionFields#filterBarMacro1\\". The same relative/absolute rule applies to a Chart: absolute binding is metaPath=\\"/<entitySet>/@com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.Chart#<qualifier>\\" (leading slash, e.g. \\"/Booking/@com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.Chart#bookingChart\\") — never a bare \\"@...Chart\\" with no entity segment. Always include a unique #qualifier in the metaPath (e.g. #filterBarMacro, #tableMacro, #bookingChart) — an unqualified annotation term can collide with an existing default (unqualified) annotation already present on the same entity in the backend metadata, in which case the auto-written annotation shell is skipped.",
    "functionalityId": "add-building-block",
  },
